(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-immunology-quiz-version-5"]={"title":"Blood & Immunology — Quiz 4 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Immunoglobulins as a whole are large plasma proteins that make up approximately what fraction of all plasma proteins, contributing meaningfully to colloid osmotic pressure?","choices":["About 2%","About 20%","Essentially 0%, since immunoglobulins circulate outside the plasma protein pool","About 60%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulins collectively make up about 20% of all plasma proteins, a large enough share that they contribute meaningfully to colloid osmotic pressure alongside albumin and other plasma proteins. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 51"},{"q":"Among the antibody isotypes, IgA is structurally notable for having how many antigen-binding sites?","choices":["4","10","1","2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. IgA has 4 antigen-binding sites, more than IgG or IgD's 2 sites each, though fewer than IgM's 10 — consistent with its role in secretions (such as mucus, tears, and saliva) where a broader binding capacity aids mucosal defense. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 51"},{"q":"B-lymphocytes undergo their initial growth and differentiation in the liver during fetal life, and after birth this role shifts to the...","choices":["bone marrow","spleen exclusively","thymus","lymph nodes exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Before birth, B-cell development occurs mainly in the fetal liver; after birth, this role shifts to the bone marrow, from which mature B-cells then migrate to the peripheral lymphoid organs. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 42"},{"q":"During T-cell development in the thymus, T-cells are tested against virtually all self-antigens, and self-reactive clones are eliminated at a strikingly high rate — up to approximately...","choices":["50%","90%","Essentially 0%, since self-reactive clones are simply suppressed, not destroyed","10%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Up to about 90% of developing T-cells that react strongly to self-antigens are destroyed during thymic selection, a critical process (occurring mostly just before and shortly after birth) that helps establish self-tolerance. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 41"},{"q":"Neutrophil margination — the sticking of neutrophils to the vessel wall during inflammation — is triggered upstream by inflammatory cytokines released at the injury site, most notably...","choices":["secretin and cholecystokinin hormones","circulating insulin and glucagon levels","tumor necrosis factor-alpha and interleukin-1","renal erythropoietin and thrombopoietin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory cytokines, especially tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α) and interleukin-1 (IL-1), initiate the cascade that increases adhesion molecule expression (selectins and ICAM-1) on endothelial cells, which then bind complementary integrins on neutrophils to produce margination. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 25"},{"q":"The adhesion molecules selectins and ICAM-1, upregulated on endothelial cells during inflammation, promote neutrophil margination by binding to complementary molecules on the neutrophil surface called...","choices":["perforins","opsonins","immunoglobulins","integrins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Selectins and ICAM-1 on activated endothelial cells bind complementary integrins on the neutrophil surface, producing the firm adhesion (margination) that precedes neutrophil migration out of the vessel into the injured tissue. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 25"},{"q":"Beyond histamine, the chemical mediators of acute inflammation include bradykinin, serotonin, prostaglandins, complement products, clotting components, and...","choices":["erythropoietin","lymphokines","gastrin","insulin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The full list of inflammatory mediators includes histamine, bradykinin, serotonin, prostaglandins, complement products, clotting components, and lymphokines, together producing vasodilation, increased capillary permeability, and granulocyte/monocyte accumulation. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 25"},{"q":"During opsonization, a pathogen is marked for phagocytosis by antibodies acting together with a specific complement cascade product, namely...","choices":["C3","Factor XIII","C1","thrombomodulin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Opsonization occurs when antibodies combine with the C3 product of the complement cascade on a pathogen's surface, marking it (as an opsonin-coated target) for more efficient recognition and ingestion by phagocytes. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 15"},{"q":"The term 'opsonin,' derived from the Greek 'to prepare for eating,' refers to a substance (such as an antibody) that, once bound to an epitope on a pathogen, functions to...","choices":["prevent phagocytes from ever approaching or recognizing the pathogen","convert the pathogen's surface into a tolerated self-antigen","attract and enable phagocytes to recognize and ingest the pathogen more effectively","directly lyse the pathogen's membrane without any phagocyte involvement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once an opsonin binds to a pathogen's surface epitope, it marks the pathogen in a way that attracts phagocytes and enables them to recognize and ingest it far more effectively than an uncoated pathogen. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 15"},{"q":"Passive immunity, such as antibodies received across the placenta or in breast milk, provides only temporary protection primarily because...","choices":["passive immunity generates its own robust population of long-lived memory B-cells","passively received antibodies are immediately destroyed the moment they are transferred","passive immunity, by its nature, always lasts substantially longer than active immunity","the recipient did not produce the antibodies themselves and has no memory cells to sustain or renew the response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because passive immunity involves receiving preformed antibodies rather than generating an active immune response, the recipient has no memory B-cells to replenish the antibody supply, so protection fades as the transferred antibodies are naturally degraded over time. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Active immunity, whether from natural infection or vaccination, is characterized by...","choices":["receiving preformed antibodies transferred directly from another individual entirely","protection that fades away within a matter of days regardless of which antigen was involved","a response that categorically never involves any memory cell formation whatsoever afterward","the recipient's own immune system generating antibodies and/or sensitized lymphocytes, including memory cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active immunity results from the body's own immune system responding to an antigen by producing antibodies and sensitized lymphocytes, including memory cells that allow a faster, stronger response upon re-exposure — in contrast to passive immunity's temporary, borrowed protection. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 39"},{"q":"A typical antibody (immunoglobulin) molecule has a characteristic Y-shape, with two identical antigen-binding tips. This structural duplication is functionally important because it allows a single antibody molecule to...","choices":["change its binding specificity independently depending on which of the two tips is used","bind only ever a single antigen molecule in total, across its entire structure","directly digest bound antigen without any complement or phagocyte involvement","bind two antigen molecules (or two epitopes) simultaneously, promoting cross-linking and agglutination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because a typical antibody has two identical antigen-binding sites, it can bind two separate antigen molecules or epitopes at once, allowing antibodies to cross-link and clump (agglutinate) multiple antigen-bearing particles together. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 50"},{"q":"The clonal selection theory explains antibody specificity by proposing that when an antigen enters the body, it...","choices":["instructs a single generic, unspecified lymphocyte to reshape its receptor to match the antigen","selectively binds and activates the small subset of pre-existing lymphocyte clones already specific for that antigen","activates every single lymphocyte throughout the entire body simultaneously and nonspecifically","is required before any lymphocyte receptor diversity can be generated in the body at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. According to clonal selection theory, the immune system already contains millions of distinct lymphocyte clones, each specific for a different antigen before ever encountering it; an antigen entering the body selectively binds and activates only the matching clone(s), driving their proliferation and differentiation. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Eosinophils are especially important in the body's defense against...","choices":["parasitic infections","fungal infections exclusively","intracellular viral infections exclusively","gram-negative bacterial infections exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils are specialized for defense against parasites (such as helminths), releasing cytotoxic substances that damage parasitic organisms too large to be phagocytosed, and they also play a role in allergic reactions. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 27"},{"q":"One direct action of the complement cascade, beyond promoting opsonization, is to...","choices":["synthesize entirely new antibodies directly against the pathogen","convert plasminogen directly into active plasmin enzyme","directly present processed antigen fragments to helper T-cells","lyse the pathogen's cell membrane directly (via the membrane attack complex)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beyond enhancing phagocytosis through opsonization, the complement cascade can directly lyse a pathogen by assembling a membrane attack complex that punctures the target cell's membrane. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 15"},{"q":"Basophils and mast cells, upon degranulation, release histamine and heparin, contributing to...","choices":["allergic and inflammatory responses","blood clotting exclusively, with no role in inflammation","antibody production directly","red blood cell production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Degranulation of basophils and mast cells releases histamine (promoting vasodilation and increased capillary permeability) and heparin, contributing to allergic reactions and the broader inflammatory response. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 28"},{"q":"A key reason the secondary immune response to a previously encountered antigen is faster and stronger than the primary response is that...","choices":["complement proteins remain permanently elevated in plasma after the first exposure","the innate immune system entirely replaces the acquired response by the second exposure","memory B- and T-cells generated during the primary response are already present and can respond immediately","the antigen itself somehow becomes biologically weaker upon the second exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. During the primary response, a subset of activated B- and T-cells persist as long-lived memory cells; on re-exposure to the same antigen, these memory cells respond immediately and vigorously, producing a faster, larger secondary response instead of needing to start clonal selection from scratch. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 40"},{"q":"As lymph passes through a lymph node, it flows through a network of sinuses lined with macrophages, which serve to...","choices":["have no cellular contents at all, functioning purely as passive fluid conduits","produce entirely new red blood cells specifically during active infection","filter out and destroy pathogens or debris before the lymph re-enters the bloodstream","convert lymph directly into blood plasma without any filtration step"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lymph nodes act as filtering stations: as lymph percolates through the sinuses, macrophages lining them phagocytose bacteria, debris, and abnormal cells, helping ensure that clean lymph (and any escaping antigen for lymphocyte surveillance) is what re-enters the bloodstream. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 9"},{"q":"Vaccination with a killed or attenuated (weakened) organism confers active immunity because it...","choices":["works only against bacterial pathogens, and can never be made effective against any viral pathogens whatsoever, regardless of preparation","provides preformed antibodies directly to the recipient, functioning the same as passive immunity does","bypasses the need for any B-cell or T-cell lymphocyte response entirely, acting through innate mechanisms alone","exposes the immune system to antigens that trigger a real primary response and memory cell formation, without causing full-blown disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A killed or attenuated organism still carries the antigens needed to trigger a genuine primary immune response — including memory B- and T-cell formation — but lacks the full virulence to cause serious disease, so the recipient gains lasting active immunity safely. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Unlike B-cells, which can recognize intact antigen directly via surface antibodies, T-cells cannot respond to free, unprocessed antigen because they...","choices":["lack any functional antigen receptor of their own on the cell surface altogether","only recognize antigen fragments displayed by MHC molecules on another cell's surface","require thousands of antigen molecules bound simultaneously to respond at all","are only found inside the thymus and never encounter antigen elsewhere"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. T-cell receptors are structurally suited to recognize small antigen fragments only when those fragments are presented in the groove of an MHC molecule on an antigen-presenting cell's surface — they cannot bind free, intact antigen floating in solution the way B-cell surface antibodies can. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 55"},{"q":"An antigen is best defined in immunology as a substance that...","choices":["can trigger a specific immune response and be specifically bound by antibodies or sensitized lymphocytes","only refers to substances that originate specifically from bacterial cell walls","is always identical in molecular structure to the antibody generated to bind it","cannot ever be recognized by circulating T-cells, only by B-cells directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An antigen is any substance (often a protein or large polysaccharide) capable of triggering a specific acquired immune response and of being specifically recognized and bound by the antibodies or sensitized lymphocytes generated against it. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 40"},{"q":"The enormous diversity of antibody specificities the body can generate from a limited antibody gene pool arises chiefly through...","choices":["somatic recombination of gene segments during lymphocyte development, creating novel antigen-receptor combinations","random, untargeted mutation occurring across every single plasma protein gene in the genome","duplication of the entire antibody gene locus anew for every new antigen ever encountered","direct genetic inheritance of one complete, unique antibody gene per possible antigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. During B- and T-lymphocyte development, gene segments encoding the antigen-binding regions undergo somatic recombination, shuffling and combining a limited set of gene segments in an enormous number of ways to generate millions of distinct antigen-receptor specificities from a relatively small underlying gene pool. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Interferons contribute to innate antiviral defense chiefly by...","choices":["serving as the body's primary antibody class specifically against viruses","stimulating additional red blood cell production during active viral infection","directly lysing virus-infected host cells themselves via pore formation","being released from virus-infected cells to warn and protect neighboring uninfected cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Virus-infected cells release interferons, which act on neighboring uninfected cells to induce an antiviral state (interfering with viral replication), helping to limit the spread of infection even before an antigen-specific response develops. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 44"},{"q":"Immunologic self-tolerance — the avoidance of an immune attack on the body's own tissues — is maintained largely through which mechanism during lymphocyte development?","choices":["Relying exclusively on physical barriers like skin to prevent self-attack","Deletion of self-reactive lymphocyte clones during their maturation","Preventing lymphocytes from ever developing antigen receptors","Permanently activating all self-reactive clones so they neutralize themselves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Self-tolerance is maintained mainly by deleting (destroying) lymphocyte clones that react strongly against self-antigens during their maturation — for T-cells, this occurs largely in the thymus, eliminating up to about 90% of self-reactive clones before they ever reach circulation. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 41"},{"q":"During the sequence of an acute inflammatory response, neutrophils typically dominate the earliest phase of cellular defense, while macrophages become more prominent later, largely because...","choices":["there is actually no real difference whatsoever in timing between neutrophil and macrophage arrival","neutrophils are pre-formed and mobilized immediately, while monocytes need time to migrate into tissue and mature into macrophages","macrophages are actually the faster cell type to arrive, though they are less numerous overall","neutrophils are actually incapable of surviving more than a few minutes at any infection site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils, already mature and stored in reserve, can be mobilized to an infection site almost immediately, producing the early wave of cellular defense; monocytes, by contrast, must first migrate into the tissue and mature into macrophages, a process that takes longer, explaining why macrophages become more prominent only later in the response. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"Humoral immunity and cell-mediated immunity differ chiefly in that humoral immunity...","choices":["relies on activated cytotoxic T-cells acting directly upon infected host cells","is categorically incapable of neutralizing toxins under any circumstances at all","is essentially identical in cellular mechanism to cell-mediated immunity overall","relies on circulating antibodies produced by B-cells/plasma cells to neutralize extracellular threats"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Humoral immunity operates through antibodies secreted by activated B-cells (plasma cells) that circulate in blood and lymph to neutralize extracellular pathogens and toxins, whereas cell-mediated immunity instead relies on activated T-cells acting directly against infected or abnormal cells. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 35"},{"q":"Among circulating white blood cells under normal conditions, the most numerous type is the...","choices":["basophil","neutrophil","monocyte","eosinophil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are the most numerous circulating white blood cell type under normal conditions, consistent with their role as the body's first and most abundant line of rapid cellular defense against bacterial infection. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 12"},{"q":"The spleen supports immunity in a manner analogous to a lymph node's filtration of lymph, except that the spleen instead filters...","choices":["cerebrospinal fluid","bile","blood","gastric secretions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. While lymph nodes filter lymph as it passes through their sinuses, the spleen performs an analogous filtering function for blood, with its macrophages (including those in the red pulp) removing pathogens, debris, and senescent blood cells/platelets directly from the bloodstream. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 24"},{"q":"Compared with a single neutrophil, an activated tissue macrophage can phagocytose...","choices":["fewer particles than a neutrophil, due to its larger size being a disadvantage","a larger number and greater variety of particles over a longer functional lifespan","nothing at all, since macrophages are fundamentally incapable of phagocytosis","only bacteria specifically, never larger debris or senescent host cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because macrophages are larger, longer-lived, and capable of repeated phagocytic cycles (unlike neutrophils, which have a shorter lifespan and more limited phagocytic capacity), an activated macrophage can engulf a greater number and wider variety of particles over its functional life. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which pairing below correctly matches a described immune defense with its correct immune category (innate vs. acquired)?","choices":["Intact skin as a physical barrier — innate immunity","Clonal selection of antigen-specific lymphocytes — innate immunity","Antibody production by B-cells — innate immunity","Memory T-cell formation — innate immunity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Physical/chemical barriers like intact skin are classic examples of innate (nonspecific) immunity, present from birth and not requiring prior exposure to a specific antigen — in contrast to acquired immunity, which is antigen-specific and develops over time (e.g., antibody production by B-cells). — Blood & Immunology, Slide 35"}]};
